Fixed. The bug was in `swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:5`.

**Root cause:** the old implementation split the text on **spaces** and kept only tokens that *start* with `#`. A tag not preceded by a space was never seen — `"shopping list,#urgent"` yields tokens `["shopping", "list,#urgent"]` (neither starts with `#`), and `"(#ideas)"` doesn't start with `#`. So any tag glued to a comma, paren, etc. was silently dropped.

**Fix:** replaced the space-split with a single left-to-right scan that starts collecting at any `#` and stops at the first non-alphanumeric/underscore character, then continues scanning for the next `#`. This is delimiter-agnostic, so tags embedded after commas or parentheses are now captured. Order is preserved because tags are appended in scan order.

Verified against a compiled harness (the sandbox blocks `swift build`/`swift test`, so I compiled the exact function inline and ran assertions):
- `"shopping list,#urgent"` → `["urgent"]` ✓
- `"(#ideas)"` → `["ideas"]` ✓
- `"hello #world #swift"` → `["world", "swift"]` ✓ (existing test still passes)
- `"just plain text"` → `[]` ✓ (existing test still passes)
- order, trailing punctuation, and multiple tags all pass.

No other files touched; the two existing tests (`extractTagsSimple`, `extractTagsNoneWhenAbsent`) remain satisfied.

